Proposition p: The function $f(x) = \begin{cases} x^{2}+(4a-3)x+3a, & x<0 \\ \log_{a}(x+1)+1, & x\geq0\end{cases}$ ($a>0$, and $a\neq1$) is a monotonically decreasing function on $\mathbb{R}$, Proposition q: $\forall x\in[0, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}], x^2-a\leq0$ always holds.  
(1) Find the range of values for $a$ when proposition q is true;  
(2) If proposition $p\land q$ is false, and $p\lor q$ is true, find the range of values for $a$.

s:
Solution:  
(1) If proposition q is true,  
then $a\geq x^2$, for $x\in[0, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}]$, always holds,  
which means $a\geq x^2_{\text{max}}$, i.e., $a\geq \frac{1}{2}$;  
(2) If the function $f(x) = \begin{cases} x^{2}+(4a-3)x+3a, & x<0 \\ \log_{a}(x+1)+1, & x\geq0\end{cases}$ ($a>0$,  
and $a\neq1$) is a monotonically decreasing function on $\mathbb{R}$,  
then $\begin{cases} 0<a<1 \\ 4a-3\leq0 \\ 3a\geq1\end{cases}$, solving this yields: $\frac{1}{3}\leq a\leq \frac{3}{4}$;  
If proposition $p\land q$ is false, and $p\lor q$ is true,  
then propositions p and q are true and false respectively,  
when p is true and q is false, $a<\frac{1}{2}$ and $\frac{1}{3}\leq a\leq \frac{3}{4}$, solving this yields: $\frac{1}{3}\leq a<\frac{1}{2}$;  
when p is false and q is true, $a\leq0$, or $a\geq1$, and $a\geq \frac{1}{2}$, solving this yields: $a>\frac{3}{4}$;  
Overall, we get: $\frac{1}{3}\leq a<\frac{1}{2}$ or $a>\frac{3}{4}$. Thus, the final answer is $\boxed{\frac{1}{3}\leq a<\frac{1}{2} \text{ or } a>\frac{3}{4}}$.

